# Line-ending policy.
#
# This is load-bearing, not cosmetic. ADLC's executor produces patches with
# `git diff` in an isolated worktree and applies them at a level barrier. On a
# machine with `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default), git rewrites line
# endings between the worktree, the index and the patch, and `git apply` then
# fails with the famously unhelpful `corrupt patch at line N`. That bug cost a
# real debugging cycle during this project's own construction.
#
# The executor defends itself directly -- it normalises patch bytes and runs git
# with `core.autocrlf=false` -- but normalising here as well means the repository
# never stores a CRLF in the first place, so contributors on any platform
# produce byte-identical diffs.

# Normalise everything git detects as text; store LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that MUST be LF regardless of platform, because something executes or
# parses them where a CR is a syntax error or an invisible argument.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.feature       text eol=lf
*.mmd           text eol=lf
*.patch         -text
*.diff          -text

# Binary evidence artifacts: never touch these.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.webm          binary
*.mp4           binary
*.zip           binary
*.gz            binary
*.pdf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary

# Generated, but committed on purpose: gh-aw compiles `.md` workflows into
# hardened `.lock.yml` Actions files and both are required at runtime. Mark them
# so they collapse in diffs without being excluded from review.
*.lock.yml      linguist-generated=true

.github/workflows/*.lock.yml linguist-generated=true